\\,(x,y)\\ne (1,0)\\}$"] [17592186604575 "Find the dot product of the vectors $u$ and $w.$\n\n$u\\cdot w=\\,$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186613214 "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-1,-1)}x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}.$"] [17592186617824 "Let $C$ be the intersection of the half-ellipsoid $z=\\sqrt{9-4{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}$ and the cylinder ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ Given that the curve $C$ is oriented counter-clockwise when viewed from above and $\\text{curl}\\,F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,0⟩,$ the circulation of $F$ along $C$ can be written as \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr=\n$$"] [17592186623649 "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the triangle spanned by $c=⟨1,0,2⟩$ and $d=⟨0,3,-1⟩.$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186624510 "Which of the following vector fields have a stream function?\n\nI. $F(x,y)=3{x}^{2}\\,i-3{y}^{2}\\,j$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=2x\\,i-2y\\,j$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=x{y}^{3}\\,i+y\\,j$"] [17592186624526 "![](images/8e/8ec6c3353156edf794d0da25ccdebb8421a2570a6ec81625c54affcafe303889.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F$ shown above. Which of the following shows the streamlines of this vector field?"] [17592186633605 "Let $g=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ t \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $h=\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ -4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$ If $g⊥h,$ then $t=${{field-1}}."] [17592186639182 "Find the Jacobian determinant of the transformation $T$ that maps the $uvw$-space into the $xyz$-space by means of the equations\n\n$$\nx=v{e}^{2u},\\,y={e}^{u}+w,\\,z=2u+w.\n$$"] [17592186645699 "Calculate the area $A$ of the parallelogram spanned by $a=⟨-9,-1,4⟩$ and $b=⟨6,-2,0⟩.$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}"] [17592186645766 "Calculate the area ${A}_{△}$ of the triangle spanned by $u=⟨2,-1,0⟩$ and $v=⟨-3,4,-2⟩.$\n\n${A}_{△}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186645768 "Calculate the area of the triangle spanned by $c=⟨-1,2,1⟩$ and $d=⟨-1,0,3⟩.$"] [17592186653588 "![](images/d7/d7185759e7ed2211fa82e39b3731969a2593645a4da90ce46289b290ff4c32fd.png)\n\nConsider the semicircle and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0\n$$"] [17592186656926 "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨-y,\\,-z,\\,-xy⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the parametric surface $S$ is defined by\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨2{u}^{2},\\,-v,\\,3uv⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain $D=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1\\}.$\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}=⟨3v,\\,-12{u}^{2},\\,-4u⟩.$*"] [17592186658970 "Let $S$ be an oriented piecewise-smooth surface bounded by the closed curve $C$ with positive orientation and parametrization $r(t)=({t}^{2}-t)\\,i+(2{t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,j+({t}^{2}-{t}^{3})\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1).$\nGiven that $F(x,y,z)=xz\\,j+k,$ evaluate the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}\\text{curl}\\,F\\cdot \\text{d}S.\n$$"] [17592186663434 "Calculate the area of the parallelogram spanned by $a=⟨-1,1,0⟩$ and $b=⟨4,1,6⟩.$"] [17592186708439 "![](images/60/6087da076e65bda5240c428ff67c570ad59ae9a8e5a5a3c9e8406184998de56a.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ is the line segment from $O(0,0)$ to $A(1,0)$, and $C_2$ is the line segment from $A(1,0)$ to $B(0,-2)$. Traverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in that order.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x+y)\\mathbf i+(2x-2y)\\mathbf j$. Given that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\frac{11}{2},\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$.\n\nFor $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r_1(t)=t\\mathbf i+0\\mathbf j$, $0\\le t\\le1$."] [17592186708440 "![](images/87/87182d625f4e669a6b2791a17f3e12c34922043b8f3deefd6b2473e8fb529886.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ is the line segment from $O(0,0)$ to $A(2,0)$, and $C_2$ is the section of the parabola $y=4-x^2$ from $A(2,0)$ to $B(0,4)$. Traverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in that order.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x+y)\\mathbf i+2x\\mathbf j$. Given that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=2,\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$."] [17592186708445 "![](images/04/0457cb819e6f39ef9c0978e356cff788e1cf5edbef9cbf844a054ab777b70eac.png)\n\nThe path $C_1$ is the line segment from $A(-2,0)$ to $B(0,1)$, and $C_2$ is the line segment from $B(0,1)$ to $D(1,1)$. Traverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in that order.\n\nLet $\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x-2y)\\mathbf i+(2x+y)\\mathbf j$. Given that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-\\frac32,\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$."] [17592186708455 "![](images/06/06f90fbcd495493bcbab5bd9aa0109e89a6ac092aabb6949c3385bce4d543cb1.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of $C_1$, $C_2$, and $C_3$. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. For the same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$, you are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=6,\\\\\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3,\\\\\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the integral along $C_2$ in the direction of its arrows.\n\n$\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=$ {{answer}}"] [17592186708457 "![](images/3e/3e0e8cf7cb700cb6d9ee074a0bb3eac1c82b24229b8d66e636121e53998fee7e.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the five segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used for every integral. Let $L_1$ follow $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$, and let $L_2$ follow $A\\to D\\to C\\to A$. You are given\n\n$$\n\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=3,\\qquad \\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-5.\n$$\n\nThe closed path $C$ follows the **outer boundary** $A\\to B\\to C\\to D\\to A$, counterclockwise. Evaluate its line integral.\n\n$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=$ {{answer}}"] [17592186708672 "![Directed segments: C_1: D to C; C_2: B to C; C_3: D to A; C_4: B to A; C_5: A to C](images/54/5480c83b384f2abd235cdb9b380bd5a75f15a0de271692c288b5c93b340231da.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. Let $L_1$ follow $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ and $L_2$ follow $A\\to D\\to C\\to A$. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=5\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to D\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. This path is the **outer boundary**. Evaluate its line integral.\n\n$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=$ {{answer}}"] [17592186708674 "![Directed segments: C_1: D to A; C_2: A to B; C_3: B to C; C_4: D to C; C_5: C to A](images/a1/a14ec792c3924723ca962f3921cd836bc3ddf012f4485bb447da2ca8cae10f15.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. Let $L_1$ follow $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ and $L_2$ follow $A\\to C\\to D\\to A$. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe closed path $C$ follows $A\\to D\\to C\\to B\\to A$ **clockwise**. This path is the **outer boundary**. Evaluate its line integral.\n\n$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=$ {{answer}}"] [17592186708679 "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: A to D; C_3: C to D; C_4: B to C; C_5: A to C](images/f3/f336318d756a33f21dba0dba2bd52aaff23d8b266cec2c4f031bf7ef695dd080.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. Let $L_1$ follow $A\\to C\\to B\\to A$ and $L_2$ follow $A\\to C\\to D\\to A$. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=4\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to D\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. This path is the **outer boundary**. Evaluate its line integral.\n\n$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=$ {{answer}}"] [17592186708680 "![Directed segments: C_1: C to B; C_2: D to A; C_3: C to D; C_4: C to A; C_5: B to A](images/ca/ca44141368bac2cbba7ced4f762c7502e41fa94dff30ce4eebf4876603feb33e.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. Let $L_1$ follow $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ and $L_2$ follow $A\\to C\\to D\\to A$. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{L_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-1\\\\\\int_{L_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to D\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. This path is the **outer boundary**. Evaluate its line integral.\n\n$\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=$ {{answer}}"] [17592186708684 "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: C to B; C_3: A to C](images/aa/aa0c4824cd83fc9db89a280bc804427adabf51610a1d62b39d1a7292ac16bb2e.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=6\\\\\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=6\\\\\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the integral along $C_3$ in the direction of its arrows.\n\n$\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=$ {{answer}}"] [17592186708685 "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: B to C; C_3: C to A](images/38/38a4b8304ca0a8122168f6e01824a13e899daaeb9b1b71e5f0320323d5ee78c1.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=0\\\\\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=0\\\\\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the integral along $C_2$ in the direction of its arrows.\n\n$\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=$ {{answer}}"] [17592186708686 "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: C to B; C_3: C to A](images/df/dff3182806adf9689792a2a8f3d048f0734637628aed2bd0622c382218821306.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to C\\to B\\to A$ **clockwise**. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=6\\\\\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\\\\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the integral along $C_1$ in the direction of its arrows.\n\n$\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=$ {{answer}}"] [17592186708694 "![Directed segments: C_1: A to B; C_2: C to B; C_3: C to A](images/df/dff3182806adf9689792a2a8f3d048f0734637628aed2bd0622c382218821306.png)\n\nThe arrows show the orientations of the segments. The same continuous vector field $\\mathbf F$ is used throughout. The closed path $C$ follows $A\\to B\\to C\\to A$ **counterclockwise**. You are given\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=14\\\\\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=4\\\\\\int_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r&=5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the integral along $C_2$ in the direction of its arrows.\n\n$\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=$ {{answer}}"] [17592186708938 "![](images/0e/0e4a78829ce8d1135d46dda1db59c968c0306937d9c5a45b24fa41648a679ea4.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(2 x)\\mathbf i+(- x + 2 y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=4,\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$."] [17592186708940 "![](images/60/6087da076e65bda5240c428ff67c570ad59ae9a8e5a5a3c9e8406184998de56a.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(1,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,-2).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(2 x - 2 y)\\mathbf i+(- x)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=1,\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$."] [17592186708956 "![](images/04/0457cb819e6f39ef9c0978e356cff788e1cf5edbef9cbf844a054ab777b70eac.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(-2,0)$ to the point $B(0,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of the line segment from the point $B(0,1)$ to the point $D(1,1).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(x + 2 y)\\mathbf i+(- y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\frac{5}{2},\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$."] [17592186708960 "![](images/73/73c6a8268a179f72611fb65e099d1249d48501e4fd4731d03747800e1944fd97.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,2)$ to the point $D(1,3).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(0)\\mathbf i+(- 2 x + y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$."] [17592186708961 "![](images/93/93c6184f39b28a057866bb02e11e5e81a7abea4ff8a9a907670dd30cfc93d557.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(1,-1)$ to the point $B(1,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,1)$ to the point $O(0,0).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(y)\\mathbf i+(- x - 2 y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=-2,\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$."] [17592186708976 "![](images/c0/c0a4f042748ddadd824bed4ee71bca9724d7ca7dcc376a0ccf7c1353d59ec4fc.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,2)$ to the point $B(-1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(-1,2)$ to the point $D(-1,0).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- x + y)\\mathbf i+(- x - y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=0,\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$.\n\nFor $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r_1(t)=(t - 2)\\mathbf i+(2)\\mathbf j$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$."] [17592186708980 "![](images/0e/0e4a78829ce8d1135d46dda1db59c968c0306937d9c5a45b24fa41648a679ea4.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(2,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(2,0)$ to the point $B(1,1).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 2 x - y)\\mathbf i+(- y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=3,\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$.\n\nFor $C_1$, use $\\mathbf r_1(t)=(2 t)\\mathbf i+(0)\\mathbf j$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$."] [17592186708993 "![](images/73/73c6a8268a179f72611fb65e099d1249d48501e4fd4731d03747800e1944fd97.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,2)$ to the point $D(1,3).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- 2 x)\\mathbf i+(x + y)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=1,\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$.\n\nFor $C_2$, use $\\mathbf r_2(t)=(1)\\mathbf i+(t + 2)\\mathbf j$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$."] [17592186708998 "![](images/c0/c0a4f042748ddadd824bed4ee71bca9724d7ca7dcc376a0ccf7c1353d59ec4fc.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(-2,2)$ to the point $B(-1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(-1,2)$ to the point $D(-1,0).$\n\nTraverse $C=C_1\\cup C_2$ in the stated order. Let\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y)=(- x - y)\\mathbf i+(2 x)\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n\\int_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r=- \\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nevaluate $\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathrm d\\mathbf r$.\n\nFor $C_2$, use $\\mathbf r_2(t)=(-1)\\mathbf i+(2 - 2 t)\\mathbf j$, with $t$ running from $0$ to $1$."]}
